+# Reminders
+
+For whoever proposes changing how members are reminded of a pickup, most often to save the cost of SMS. The day
+before pickup, larder sends the member a reminder of their pickup slot by SMS, not email, because when the reminders
+went by email for three months, missed pickups doubled. Written after the third proposal in a year to switch to email
+([issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md)).
+
+## How a reminder is sent
+
+`remind` in `larder/reminders.py` sends "Your pantry order is ready for pickup" with the slot, the day before pickup.
+`CHANNEL` picks SMS or email; it is `"sms"`.
+
+## Why SMS, not email
+
+SMS reminders cost about 40 euros a month, and email is free, so switching keeps being proposed. It has been tried:
+
+- From April to June 2025 the reminders went by email. The email provider's report showed 34% of them were never
+  opened, and missed pickups rose from 6% of pantry orders to 13%.
+- A missed pickup is a bag already packed, and the fresh food in it is thrown away at closing time.
+- Many members share one phone in the family and never check email; they do see an SMS.
+- Reminders went back to SMS on 1 July 2025, and by August missed pickups were back to 6%.
+
+A proposal to switch to email should answer the rise in missed pickups first: the saving is 40 euros a month against
+the fresh food thrown away from 7 more pantry orders in every 100.
